AGENTS.md and the Wave-5 notebooks describe INV-zogud's ratchet as "BLOCKING,
instrument-pinned to mypy 2.1.x". On the pipeline that gates merges it was
neither, and nothing ran mypy locally at all.

THE GATING PIPELINE IS WOODPECKER, AND ITS RATCHET STEP COULD NOT FAIL.
auto-pr polls exactly one CI context -- ci/woodpecker/pr/woodpecker -- and
merges on it; observed live on PR #204. The GitHub Actions --mode=strict job
and its ci-complete gate are never consulted by the merge path. That
Woodpecker step ran --mode=warning AND carried `failure: ignore`, so it could
not gate by either route.

THREE EDITS WERE REQUIRED AND TWO ARE SILENT ALONE.
  * `failure: ignore` removed. With it set, --mode=strict exits 1 and
    Woodpecker passes the step anyway -- so flipping the mode by itself reads
    as a fix and gates exactly nothing.
  * --mode=warning -> --mode=strict. Warning mode exits 0 on a regression.
  * mypy~=2.1 -> mypy~=2.1.0, matching ci.yml, whose comment already explains
    why: a baseline's counts are only comparable against the mypy that
    produced them, and under the loose spec a fresh install can resolve
    2.2/2.3, at which point the ratchet refuses to compare -- silently, under
    the old warning mode.

SMART-TEST RUNS THE RATCHET FIRST, and a regression forbids testing. Before
slicing and before pytest. On a strict failure it prints the breach and exits
1 WITHOUT running pytest. The message closes the specific exit that let this
happen: you may not run tests until it is clean, EVEN IF YOU DID NOT CAUSE THE
REGRESSION. "Not my change" is not an exit -- the ratchet is shrink-only, so
whoever observes the breach owns it, and a breach every agent in turn declines
to own is exactly how the surface drifted 672 -> 682 across sixty commits
touching 24 source files. The banner also forbids the two known routes around
it (direct pytest, HG_SKIP_MYPY_GATE) and forbids "fixing" a breach by raising
the baseline, which a shrink-only ratchet may never do (L33).

Exit-code policy follows check-mypy-ratchet's own documented contract: exit 1
(regression, or an instrument mismatch, which means the gate cannot do its
job) blocks; exit 2 (infrastructure -- mypy missing, no source roots) warns and
continues, because "an infra failure is not a type regression".

VERIFIED END TO END, NOT JUST ASSERTED. With .ci/mypy-strict-baseline.json
doctored to zeros, smart-test exits 1, prints the block banner, and produces
ZERO test-result lines -- pytest never runs. Baseline restored and confirmed
clean against git afterwards.

The two silent-failure assertions are MUTATION-TESTED: restoring
`failure: ignore` fails exactly test_mypy_step_does_not_ignore_failure, and
loosening the pin fails exactly the pin test. Neither is a passenger.

TWO WRONG DRAFTS OF ONE TEST, RECORDED IN PLACE RATHER THAN QUIETLY REPLACED.
"No invocation uses warning mode" first grepped raw file text and failed on
all three files -- each one DOCUMENTS why warning mode is wrong, so the
forbidden string appears in the prose explaining the prohibition. The second
draft dropped comments and then flagged `check-schema-coverage --mode=warning`,
a DIFFERENT tool that is legitimately advisory: that version would have
forbidden correct configuration, which is worse than not testing at all. The
shipped version takes the invocation as its unit, not the file.

HONEST LIMIT: the gate lives in smart-test, which is what the pytest alias
invokes, so it covers the normal path. A direct `python -m pytest` bypasses
it; there the enforcement is the banner text, not a mechanism.

